# Private working material. scripts/audit_open_source.py refuses a release
# that ships any of these, so tracking one of them turns the open-source gate
# red on every push; the gate was correct and the files were the violation.
# They stay on disk -- this only takes them out of the index.
/docs/superpowers/
/deliverables/
**/.comet/

# Stray local binaries from `go build ./scripts/<tool>`. One line per
# incident is how this list got to three: the ignore rule does not stop the
# build, and a checker that reads the tree does (scripts/modulecheck,
# checkNoRootBuildArtifacts).
/modulecheck
/deadcode
/domaincheck
/brokerpin
/cigate
/cochange
/nodearch

# Root-level binaries from `go build ./scripts/<name>/`.
#
# Every script target in the Makefile runs `go run`, which leaves the build
# product in the cache and nothing in the tree. A `go build` typed by hand
# while checking a change drops a Mach-O file at the repository root, where
# `bin/` does not cover it and no existing rule matches -- one was committed
# by accident before this rule existed. The list is the `package main`
# directories under scripts/; a new one is not covered until it is added here,
# so `git status` after building one is the check that catches it.
/agentteams-plugin-manager
/apidoc
/brokerarch
/brokerpin
/cigate
/cochange
/dashboard
/deadcode
/deadpkg
/domaincheck
/e2e_real
/ledgercheck
/modulecheck
/nodearch
/opskeeper
/routeaudit
/rpcmatch
/splitprice
/tablecheck
/transcheck

# Local run artifacts. backend.env holds a JWT secret and an admin password,
# and backend.log plus the built binary are per-machine output -- none of it
# belongs in the index. A `git add -A` before this line existed put all three
# into a commit; it was caught before it was pushed (decision 420).
.devrun/
.worktrees/
